References: <E42411ED-1F58-48E7-A980-BC4F4B5C0372@tcd.ie> <alpine.OSX.2.00.0903161006500.9710@pol-g88381xtxyl.Princeton.EDU> <4E446873-B4FA-4248-AF49-36DACA5BB428@tcd.ie>
Message-ID: <alpine.OSX.2.00.0903161044030.9710@pol-g88381xtxyl.Princeton.EDU>

Either way is fine, I think. The MLE is exp(E(XB)) and the posterior mean 
is E(exp(XB)).  The former can also be approximated by the posterior 
median.  If you report the MLE, you need to use the delta method to give 
confidence intervals, etc. which may not be fun.  Alternatively, you can 
report Bayesian confidence intervals by just taking the 2.5th and 97.5th 
percentile of your simulation draws.  You can also report either the 
posterior mean or posterior median.

Kosuke

-- 
Department of Politics
Princeton University
http://imai.princeton.edu

